The	Ownership	Module
ARCOS	—	Specification,	Testing	&	Governance	Framework
The	Ownership	Module
This	is	the	Ownership	Module	—	the	first,	and	so	far	only,	Module	built
out	to	full	depth	against	the	general	Module	architecture.	Everything
specific	in	this	document	(the	three-field	state,	the	Operations,	the
guardian	mechanism)	is	Ownership’s	own	answer	to	the	six
architectural	questions	—	not	a	template	the	other	planned	Modules
are	required	to	repeat.
The	Core	Move
ownerOf()	on	ERC-721	is	a	single,	mutable	pointer	—	no	rights
structure,	no	enforcement,	no	history,	no	recovery.
The	Ownership	Module	sits	on	top	of	it	(reading	it,	not	overwriting	it,
in	the	retrofit	form)	and	turns	that	pointer	into	a	governed	claim:
decomposable	into	rights,	changeable	only	through	authorized	paths,
provable	after	the	fact,	recoverable	if	the	holder	is	lost.
State
Not	one	value	—	three:
Possession	—	who	holds	it
Use	—	who	may	use	it
Benefit	—	who	gets	its	value
These	move	independently	or	together	—	that	decomposition	is	the
reason	a	bundle	beats	a	pointer.
Each	field	carries	exactly	one	active	claim	at	a	time	—	two	Operations
cannot	both	hold	Use	simultaneously	(mutual-exclusivity	rule,	closes	a
race	condition	the	earlier	flat	model	had).
Authority
One	root	address	is	the	sole	thing	allowed	to	authorize	a	change	to
any	field.	Every	Operation	checks	against	this	before	touching	state.
Authority	is	a	passive	reference	value	—	MAS	is	what	evaluates	the
check	and	commits	the	result.
