Invariants	/	Rules
Split	into	two	layers:
Universal	—	only	the	Authority	root	may	act;	all	Operations	freeze
while	the	root	is	lost.
Local	to	one	Operation	—	e.g.	Shared	Ownership’s	shares	must
sum	to	100%;	no	other	Operation	cares	about	this	rule.
Most	rules	are	local,	not	universal	—	confirmed	after	testing	whether
a	single	global	rule	set	held	anything	a	per-Operation	rule	didn’t.
Open	question:	whether	this	universal/local	split	is	general	to	all
Modules	or	specific	to	Ownership	—	untested	against	a	second
domain.
Lifecycle	&	History
History	is	append-only,	field-level,	tagged	by	which	Operation	caused
the	change.	Never	overwrites.	This	is	what	makes	disputes	resolvable
—	current	state	and	how	it	was	reached	are	both	reconstructible.
Lifecycle	is	not	a	stored	field	—	it’s	a	computed	read	(Minted	→
current	stage)	derived	live	from	state	+	History.	It	earns	a	place	as	a
display,	not	as	governed	data	—	a	digital	asset	has	no	independent
aging	process,	only	recorded	transitions.
Failure	/	Recovery
Guardian	vote	(2-of-3)	reassigns	the	Authority	root	when	it’s	lost.	The
one	transition	authorized	by	something	other	than	the	current	root.
Scoped	narrowly:	only	the	root	pointer	changes.	Active	Operation
claims	survive	a	root	change	—	the	way	a	lease	survives	a	change	of
landlord.
This	mechanism	is	Ownership-specific,	not	universal	—	see	the
Module	document.
System	Interaction	—	MAS
MAS	is	the	actual	executor	of	every	check	above,	running	off-chain
today.	It	orchestrates	what	the	Solidity/JS	logic	can’t	yet	do	natively
on	a	retrofit	anchor.
Where	This	Sits	in	ARCOS
Ownership	is	one	of	eight	planned	Core	Modules	(Ownership,
Transfer,	Permission,	Metadata,	Verification,	Royalty,	Composition,
Security).
Runs	under	a	native-vs-retrofit	enforcement	model	—	only	native
deployment	(mint-time	hooks)	closes	the	transferFrom()	bypass
gap;	retrofit	can	read	state	but	not	stop	a	direct	call	from
bypassing	it.
Ownership	specifically	has	4	of	6	rights-bundle	capabilities	with
zero	prior	ERC	coverage	—	it	fills	a	real	absence,	it	doesn’t
compete	with	an	existing	standard.
Open,	Unresolved	Points
MAS	is	the	actual	trust	root,	not	Authority.	Every	check,
rejection,	and	recorded	transition	is	only	as	honest	as	whoever
operates	MAS	—	today,	that’s	ARCOS.	Until	there’s	a	credible	path
to	a	federated	or	verifiable	operator	set,	the	accurate	claim	is
“well-governed	permissioned	service,”	not	“trustless
infrastructure.”
Benefit	is	a	recorded	intention,	not	a	settled	transfer.	Rental
logs	“20%	to	renter”	—	no	value	actually	moves	yet.	Recording	the
entitlement	and	settling	the	value	behind	it	are	separate,	and	only
the	first	is	built.
The	retrofit	gap	is	structural.	A	direct	transferFrom()	call	on	the
base	contract	bypasses	this	entire	Module.	Every	Operation,	every
Rule,	every	guardian	vote	is	voluntarily	observed,	not	enforced,
until	this	exists	as	a	native	contract.
Guardian	mechanism	is	a	placeholder	—	fixed	3-guardian	list,
not	a	real	design.
Recovery’s	interaction	with	active	Operations	is	unresolved
—	what	happens	to	a	live	Delegation/Custody/Rental	record	when
Recovery	reassigns	the	root	has	not	been	decided.
Status
Built	and	validated:	three-field	rights	bundle,	Authority-gate
pattern,	mutual-exclusivity	rule,	universal/local	invariant	split,
append-only	field-level	History,	Lifecycle	as	a	computed	read,
Recovery	scoped	to	touch	only	the	root	pointer.
Prototyped,	not	enforced	on-chain:	v0.5	is	a	TypeScript	prototype;
MAS	runs	off-chain;	ModuleRegistry.sol	and	ARCOSAnchor.sol	are
storage/read	scaffolding,	not	deployed	enforcement	contracts.
Open	and	concrete	—	a	place	to	start	if	this	is	a	problem	worth
working	on:	the	guardian	mechanism,	and	Recovery-vs-active-Operations-interaction.
